  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 128 PM CDT Fri Aug 7 2026\n\nRain/storm chances...Risk for severe weather has been upgraded to\na slight risk for north-central Wisconsin, with a marginal risk \nfor the rest of the region this afternoon as compact shortwave \nand weak cold front traverse the upper Mississippi Valley. CAM \nconsensus continues to show a broken line of convection developing\nover northwest Wisconsin along the front during peak heating \n(1,500 to 2,000 J/kg MLCAPE), progressing over the Northwoods \nthrough the evening. However, biggest question will be whether or \nnot the brunt of the severe action splits us to the north, where \nbetter deep-layer shear resides closer to the shortwave, and to \nthe southwest where better moisture return is. NCAR ML guidance \nshows higher probs over these areas as an artifact of previous CAM\nruns. Given the general consensus of the CAMs bringing these\nstorms through north-central Wisconsin, the higher risk for severe\nweather appears appropriate. Any strong to severe storms would\nmainly be a wind threat due to stout inverted-V profiles tapping \ninto 30+ knot winds at the top of the dry layer, with a lesser but\nnon-zero threat for large hail. Severe threat and confidence \ndiminishes further east where the marginal risk resides.\n\nNot many changes to speak of beyond tonight as several embedded \nshortwaves riding the 500 mb wave train bring multiple rounds of \nshowers and storms Saturday night through early next week. Most of\nthe moisture/instability look to remain holed up to our southwest\nunder western ridge; as such, better chances for more organized \nconvection remain where better thermodynamics are. However, period\nto keep an eye on will be Sunday into Monday as surface low spins\nup over the Plains and signal for better instability exists over \nthe western Great Lakes. ML guidance currently shows 15 to 30% \nprob severe into portions of central Wisconsin during this time. \nLikewise, heavy rain threat remains conditional and will likely \nhinge on training storms where the boundary stalls out. As it \nappears now, axis of heaviest precip Sunday into Monday looks to \ntarget southern Wisconsin, with more of a glancing blow to our \nsouthern tier of counties. WPC carries Marginal ERO on days 4 and \n5. \n\nTemperatures/smoke...Temperatures to read near seasonal norms in \nthe low 80s through the better part of next week. Elevated smoke \nplume works into northeast Wisconsin preceding cold FROPA tonight,\nthough no significant impacts to visibility or air quality are \nexpected. Elevated smoke to return late Saturday into Sunday.",
